  "query_preview": "..\nThe upper pattern this morning is defined by a strong ridge of \nhigh pressure extending from eastern New Mexico into western \nIllinois, with a high center positioned over ERN Kansas. The \nlatest GOES precipitable water imagery indicated the deeper \nmoisture resides from Tucson west (PWATs between 1.50 and 1.75 \ninches) and NW Sonora Mexico with generally drier air with PWAT \nvalues 1.30 to 1.50 inches to the east of Tucson into SW New \nMexico and NW Chihuahua MX. \n\nAn inverted positioned along the base of the upper ridge this \nmorning will track west today amplifying the flow and resulting\nin a secondary high center developing over southern New Mexico \nby 09/00Z this afternoon. Not much large scale lift across\nSoutheast Arizona today as the upper ridge nudges into the area\nfrom the east, but there is a small area of deformation aloft \nthat develop along the International Border SE of Tucson. The \nlack of mid-level flow will result in mainly mainly isolated \npulse-type thunderstorms today with the primary hazard being \nlocalized heavy rain due to the fact PWATs are approximately \n130-150% of normal for this time of year.\n\nI think the primary weather focus should be on Wednesday (and \nto a lesser extent Thursday) since the days that are the most \nactive usually occur when the upper high center is on the move. \nThe upper high center is progged to move into central Arizona by\n10/00Z Wednesday afternoon then WRN AZ by 10/06Z. This movement\nresults in an area of decent stretching deformation/lifting \nmechanism aloft across Southeast Arizona while also providing a \nfavored rim-shot pattern to move storms from the Mogollon Rim SW\ntoward the Tucson Metro Area. The presence of 25-30 kts of \n0-6km AGL Bulk Shear Wednesday evening across the eastern half \nof the forecast area will help organize and cluster storms as \nthey move into eastern Pima county. Deeper moisture moving into \nPima/SE Pinal and Santa Cruz counties from NW Sonora Mexico \nduring the day Wednesday will provide the potential for heavy \nrainfall/isolated flash flooding. Could be an interesting day. \n\nOtherwise, we will maintain enough moisture across the region\nfor mainly scattered afternoon and early evening thunderstorms\nthis weekend into early next week. The Monsoon season rolls-on.",
